From d9814552e37704927075c46d44efcc30ac052b74 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 11:17:33 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] adapter: switching the radio off stops the poll loop
 first

The kernel will not disable an adapter that is still polling, and
set_powered() passes that refusal straight back to the caller. What a
user sees is a switch that will not switch: with any application
scanning, the NFC toggle in GNOME Settings springs back on every time,
and a quick settings tile only works if it stops the loop itself and
then wins the race against the application restarting it.

Turning a radio off is not a request to be refused because something is
using it. Stop the loop in the one place every client goes through, so
that off means off however the caller got there.
---
 src/adapter.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/adapter.c b/src/adapter.c
index 60318c3..fd7af87 100644
--- a/src/adapter.c
+++ b/src/adapter.c
@@ -251,6 +251,27 @@ static void set_powered(GDBusPendingPropertySet id, dbus_bool_t powered,
 	struct near_adapter *adapter = data;
 	int err;
 
+	/*
+	 * The kernel refuses to disable an adapter that is still polling, and
+	 * that refusal reaches the user as a switch that springs back on: the
+	 * NFC toggle in GNOME Settings cannot turn the radio off at all while
+	 * any application is scanning, and a quick settings tile has to ask
+	 * twice. Every client would otherwise need to know to stop the loop
+	 * first, and to win the race against whichever application restarts it.
+	 *
+	 * Turning a radio off is not a request to be refused because something
+	 * is using it. Stop the loop here, so that switching off means
+	 * switching off however the caller got here.
+	 */
+	if (!powered && adapter->polling) {
+		DBG("stopping the poll loop so the adapter can be disabled");
+
+		if (__near_netlink_stop_poll(adapter->idx) == 0) {
+			adapter->polling = false;
+			polling_changed(adapter);
+		}
+	}
+
 	err = __near_netlink_adapter_enable(adapter->idx, powered);
 	if (err < 0) {
 		if (err == -EALREADY) {
